From: portare-ch <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] audio: audio_out_rate = 0 picks the output rate from the core's

The output rate was one number for every core, and the device's link
was reopened at it whatever the core produced: a 32040 Hz SNES or a
22050 Hz N64 game went through the resampler to 48000. Per-core config
files can name a rate, but the N64 has none to name: each game programs
its own into the AI, and the core reports it through
SET_SYSTEM_AV_INFO once the game has, which already reinitialises the
audio driver.

With audio_out_rate = 0 the init picks the output rate from the rates
the link carries natively (32000, 44100, 48000 on the Nova): the
smallest one the core's rate divides into within 0.5 % (32040 -> 32000,
22050 -> 44100, 44100 -> 44100), else the smallest above it so nothing
is cut (32768 -> 44100), else the highest (65536 -> 48000). The pick
happens at every init, so the N64's reopen lands on the game's rate.
The setting stays 0; a non-zero audio_out_rate, from the main config or
a per-core override, is used as before.

Everything that read the setting for the device's rate reads the rate
in use instead: the stats overlay, the latency figures, the mixer, the
refresh-rate ratio and RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_TARGET_SAMPLE_RATE, which
before the first init answers with the pick for the core's rate.
---
 audio/audio_driver.c | 106 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----------
 audio/audio_driver.h |   4 ++
 gfx/video_driver.c   |   6 +--
 retroarch.c          |   2 +-
 runloop.c            |   2 +-
 5 files changed, 95 insertions(+), 25 deletions(-)
